# Claura

**Tagline:** Your network is compromised. Your command isn't.

**Elevator pitch (short field):** The immune system for AI data centers: on-prem NVIDIA Nemotron agents contain a breached GPU host under policy, while an isolated command center keeps every human on one live record.

**Links:** Try it: https://claura.tech · Code: https://github.com/zero-abd/claura · Video: [paste the public link]

**Tracks:** Winstead PC (Cyber Incident Response Dashboard) · Spacetime (Build on Spacetime) · NVIDIA (Use Nemotron Beyond a Chatbot). Built with NMC²'s "Keep the World Running" problems in mind too: missed warnings, unclear handoffs, wrong information at the wrong time.

> **What's live and what's simulated.** Live: the console, the SpacetimeDB module on Maincloud (all incident state, identities and roles, the policy gate, War Room, data scope, documentation), four agents driven by Nemotron running locally, and the Secure Shell certificate flow. Simulated: the attack telemetry is a replayed fixture, containment changes state in the database rather than touching real hypervisors, and the shell targets are real OpenSSH daemons in sandboxes standing in for GPU hosts. The 9.4 s, 2,048 hosts and 61,207 agent sandboxes on screen are illustrative.

---

## Inspiration

Winstead's track brief asked us to assume the worst: the attacker can see normal communications, ransomware has locked the data, phones are down, email is sporadic, and nobody has information. That isn't paranoia. CISA and the FBI document a crew, Scattered Spider, that searches victims' Slack, Teams and Exchange for conversations about the response and "frequently join[s] incident remediation and response calls" ([CISA AA23-320A](https://www.cisa.gov/news-events/cybersecurity-advisories/aa23-320a)). Every tool in a normal response (the SIEM, the tickets, the chat, the bridge call, the runbook on the file share, the long-lived SSH keys) lives on the network the attacker owns. You don't investigate in front of the suspect.

Then we put that night inside an AI data center. A GPU host there carries many customers' workloads, and the wall between them is software that cracks. On 30 Sep 2026 NVIDIA disclosed CVE-2026-47498, which lets a user inside a guest VM trigger an out-of-bounds write in the host's vGPU Manager ([NVD](https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-47498)). A year earlier, Wiz's NVIDIAScape showed a three-line Dockerfile taking root on a GPU host ([The Hacker News](https://thehackernews.com/2025/07/critical-nvidia-container-toolkit-flaw.html)). The attackers are speeding up too: in a campaign Anthropic disrupted, AI did 80 to 90 percent of the work ([Anthropic](https://www.anthropic.com/news/disrupting-AI-espionage)), and the fastest breakout CrowdStrike recorded in 2025 took 27 seconds ([CrowdStrike](https://www.crowdstrike.com/en-us/press-releases/2026-crowdstrike-global-threat-report/)).

So we set three rules. The first responder has to be a machine. The last word has to be a human. Everything in between has to be policy. And the command post has to live outside the crime scene.

## What it does

Claura is the immune system for AI data centers. In our scenario, one customer's AI workload tries to break out of its virtual machine toward the shared GPU host where eleven other tenants are working. Claura detects it, maps the blast radius, contains it under policy, gets one human approval, and keeps every stakeholder coordinated and documented somewhere the attacker can't read.

**The machine side**
- **Overview.** A real-time 3D data center you can click: halls, racks and hosts, with the suspect host lighting up.
- **Four defender agents on NVIDIA Nemotron, on-prem.** Sentry correlates six weak signals (an IOMMU fault, a vGPU driver fault, qemu spawning a shell, a flow to the management port and more) and opens the incident. Triage queries host inventory and reachability and records the blast radius: one host, eleven co-resident neighbors. Warden proposes containment, reversible steps first: isolate the network, revoke credentials, cordon the host, pause the guest, capture memory, fail the neighbors over to clean hosts. Scribe writes the plain-language brief and the shift handoff.
- **A policy gate the model can't talk its way past.** Every agent action is a proposal to a SpacetimeDB reducer, which rules from the policy table: auto, needs approval, or denied. Unknown actions and hosts that don't exist fail closed. A labelled red-team probe asks to reimage the host, and the gate blocks it. Policy, not the model, decides.
- **One human step.** Quarantining the host needs a person: evidence, scope and rollback on one card, approved with Cmd+Enter.

**The human side (Winstead's five requirements)**

| Requirement | What Claura does |
|---|---|
| Highly secure, isolated inside a compromised network | The command plane runs outside the network it defends, on SpacetimeDB. It needs no corporate SSO, email, phones or DNS. Every reducer checks the caller's identity, and only admins can add people or change policy. |
| Access control, admin adds individuals | **People & Access:** the admin adds someone (outside counsel, say) with a role by issuing a single-use break-glass enrollment code. No email, no phone, no SSO. The access event lands in the audit log. |
| Documentation to incident standards, real-time status | **Reports:** the incident record is structured to NIST SP 800-61, with live phase status, a SHA-256 chain of custody for evidence, and PDF export for counsel. |
| Scope of compromised data and its status | **Data Scope:** every data asset carries a status: intact, accessed, exfiltration suspected, exfiltration confirmed, locked by ransomware, restored, or evidence preserved. |
| Real-time communications | **War Room:** channels for #command, #legal, #exec, #comms-pr and #vendors; Scribe's brief ("what we know / what we don't know yet"); tasks with owners; a decision log with a name on every decision; example notification clocks labelled "confirm with counsel". Every console updates live. |

**Secure Shell.** Click a host and Claura's own SSH certificate authority mints a just-in-time OpenSSH user certificate that expires within minutes. The host presents a CA-signed host certificate, so there's no trust-on-first-use. A passkey or Touch ID step-up is required, a suspect host needs a second approver and accepts only a read-only forensic principal, and every session is recorded into a hash-chained ledger.

## How we built it

```
 on-prem (next to the hardware)                  out-of-band (outside the corporate network)
 ┌─────────────────────────────────┐             ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────┐
 │ Agent runner (Node + TypeScript) │  reducers   │ SpacetimeDB module on Maincloud               │
 │ Nemotron 3 Nano 4B via Ollama    │ ──────────▶ │ tables: hosts, guests, signals, incidents,    │
 │ Sentry · Triage · Warden · Scribe│ ◀────────── │ findings, proposals, policy, operators, roles,│
 └─────────────────────────────────┘ subscriptions│ War Room messages, data scope, evidence,      │
 ┌─────────────────────────────────┐             │ reports                                       │
 │ Secure Shell gateway             │             │ reducers: policy gate, approvals, access      │
 │ SSH CA · passkeys · ledger       │             │ control, documentation                        │
 └─────────────────────────────────┘             └──────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                                                        ▲ live subscriptions
                                         Liquid Glass consoles (every operator, every device)
```

- **SpacetimeDB is the backend.** One TypeScript module on Maincloud holds every piece of incident state, plus operator identities, roles and the War Room. The policy gate lives in the `propose_action` reducer: in one transaction it reads the policy row and validates the target, then writes the proposal as executed, pending approval, or blocked. A refused action never commits, so it can't run. Admin-only reducers handle enrollment and policy changes, and every console is a live subscription, which is how two laptops show the same incident at the same moment. The only processes outside SpacetimeDB are the two that must sit next to hardware: the on-prem agent runner and the SSH gateway.
- **Nemotron agents, beyond a chatbot.** A Node and TypeScript runner drives NVIDIA Nemotron 3 Nano (`nemotron-3-nano:4b`, 2.9 GB) through Ollama on local hardware. No model API sits in the loop. Four agents share nine tools (`list_recent_signals`, `open_incident`, `get_host_inventory`, `check_reachability`, `record_finding`, `get_policy`, `propose_action`, `list_events`, `file_report`), and every effect is a reducer call. The loop has real guardrails: JSON-schema validation of every argument, one retry with the error fed back to the model, stall detection, step and time limits, completion judged by database state rather than by the model's say-so, and a deterministic fallback per agent. Every output is tagged `model`, `model+fallback` or `fallback`, so you can always see what Nemotron did.
- **The console.** TypeScript and Vite, with a Liquid Glass design and progressive disclosure: calm when nothing is wrong, one card when it needs you. The 3D data center is three.js and deterministic, so the film and the live view match.
- **Secure Shell.** A Node, Express and WebSocket gateway with xterm.js in the browser, passkeys through SimpleWebAuthn, and OpenSSH certificates signed by `ssh-keygen`: one principal per user certificate and a 15-minute maximum. Each target `sshd` accepts only the principals in its `AuthorizedPrincipalsFile` (no keys, no passwords), and presents a host certificate signed by Claura's host CA, which the client trusts instead of trusting on first use. The session ledger chains each entry to the previous one with SHA-256, so editing history breaks every hash after it.
- **Brand and film.** claura.tech and the mark (a C-shaped aura ring whose seal bar closes the gap), and a demo film built with HyperFrames.

## Challenges we ran into

- **Making a 4B model a reliable tool user.** Early runs produced malformed arguments and early stops. Schema checks with readable errors, a single retry, and judging "done" from the database fixed it. In one live run Nemotron wrote a finding shorter than the minimum, was told why, and fixed it on the retry.
- **Honesty about the forbidden action.** We wanted to show the gate refusing a destructive step. Across four prompt variants, Nemotron never proposed one on its own. Instead of faking it, we ship a labelled red-team probe and show the gate blocking it.
- **Isolation without the usual identity stack.** If SSO, email and phones are compromised, you can't use them to invite people. Single-use break-glass enrollment codes and identities held in SpacetimeDB solved it.
- **SSH without trust-on-first-use.** That meant a host CA as well as a user CA, short certificate lifetimes, and a principal model where a suspect host accepts only the read-only forensic role.
- **Data scope is not infrastructure scope.** "One host, eleven guests" says nothing about whether a dataset was encrypted or copied. We modelled data assets and their status separately.

## Accomplishments that we're proud of

- The full loop runs live on a laptop: in one logged run Nemotron made 15 turns and 14 tool calls with zero malformed calls, every action ruled by the policy gate, then contained after a human approval.
- Changing one policy row changes behavior with no code change: set quarantine to auto and the same proposal executes with no human; set it back and it waits.
- All five Winstead requirements in one console, usable under stress: one card, one keystroke, one plain-language brief.
- Two consoles, one truth, with no refresh.
- Real OpenSSH certificates and a tamper-evident session ledger behind a single click.

## What we learned

- **Policy, not the model, decides.** A deterministic gate is what makes a small, local model safe to act on production systems.
- **Incident response is a coordination problem.** Detection gets the headlines, but the night is lost when people can't talk safely or can't agree on what's true. The communications plane has to sit outside the blast radius.
- **Plain language is a security feature.** "What we know / what we don't know yet" stops guessing from spreading.
- **Label the simulated parts.** It cost us nothing, and it makes everything we show live more believable.

## What's next for Claura

All of this is roadmap and not built yet.

- **Real senses:** hypervisor, IOMMU, vGPU, EDR, BMC and identity connectors, with design partners measuring time-to-contain and false-positive rates to replace our illustrative numbers.
- **The agent firewall:** the same policy gate in front of every AI agent's tool calls in the building.
- **Autonomous recovery:** reimage, re-attest and return hosts to the pool, with a human signing once.
- **DPU enforcement:** containment on NVIDIA BlueField-class DPUs, below the hypervisor, so a compromised host can't undo its own quarantine.
- **Confidential-computing attestation:** prove a destination host and GPU are clean before any tenant fails over onto it.
- **Insurance and compliance evidence:** signed proof-of-containment packs for auditors, regulators and cyber insurers.
- **Cross-cloud threat sharing on SpacetimeDB:** operators exchange signed "antibodies" (escape fingerprints and policy rules, never tenant data), so every breach makes the whole fleet immune.
- **Sovereign on-prem defense:** an air-gapped edition with on-prem Nemotron and self-hosted SpacetimeDB for national AI compute and critical infrastructure.

Why it matters: McKinsey projects $6.7 trillion of data-center capex by 2030, $5.2 trillion of it for AI ([McKinsey](https://www.mckinsey.com/industries/technology-media-and-telecommunications/our-insights/the-cost-of-compute-a-7-trillion-dollar-race-to-scale-data-centers)). Our first customers, multi-tenant GPU clouds, passed $25 billion in revenue in 2025 ([Synergy Research](https://srgresearch.com/articles/neocloud-market-forecast-to-approach-400b-by-2031-driven-by-surging-ai-infrastructure-demand)). The average breach now costs a record $4.99 million ([IBM via Baker Donelson](https://www.bakerdonelson.com/ten-takeaways-from-ibms-2026-cost-of-a-data-breach-report)).

## Built with

spacetimedb · maincloud · nvidia-nemotron · nemotron-3-nano · ollama · typescript · node.js · vite · three.js · openssh · webauthn · simplewebauthn · passkeys · xterm.js · express · websockets · hyperframes
# HackUTD 2026 Judge Panel Review: Claura

## Executive verdict

The strongest direction is a hybrid:

- Use **Crime Scene** for the opening problem: “The attacker is reading your email.”
- Use **Immune System** as the durable product category and closing metaphor.
- Use **Agents vs. Agents** to prove Nemotron’s necessity.
- Borrow only the “seal the compartment” concept from **Bulkheads**; the Titanic history consumes too much of a two-minute pitch.

The single biggest risk is credibility. The drafts repeatedly describe the policy gate, Maincloud War Room, authentication, Secure Shell, and voice as real while their truth checks say some may be static, unwired, or absent. A judge discovering that gap will punish the project more severely than if you disclose it upfront.

Scores below assume you demonstrate only what is actually live. If you state an unchecked feature as built, subtract 2–4 points with the security, legal, NVIDIA, and SpacetimeDB judges.

Draft abbreviations:

- **Immune:** `immune-system.md`
- **Bulkheads:** `bulkheads.md`
- **Crime Scene:** `crime-scene.md`
- **Agents:** `agents-vs-agents.md`

---

# 1. CISO / veteran security engineer

## Scores

| Draft | Score | One-sentence verdict |
|---|---:|---|
| Immune | 9/10 | The best safety story—deterministic policy, reversible-first actions, evidence, and explicit limitations—but “irreversible quarantine” contradicts its own rollback claims. |
| Bulkheads | 7/10 | Damage containment is the right concept, but the extended ship analogy obscures threat boundaries, authentication, and actual enforcement. |
| Crime Scene | 8/10 | Strongest treatment of compromised communications and evidentiary risk, although the control plane’s isolation is asserted more than architecturally demonstrated. |
| Agents | 8/10 | Clear adversarial-speed argument and useful fail-closed model, but “agents versus agents” invites skepticism about false positives and prompt injection. |

### First place: Immune System

It frames the core security design correctly: the model proposes, deterministic controls authorize, reversible actions precede disruptive ones, and humans retain responsibility. Its Q&A also admits unmeasured false-positive rates and prompt-injection testing gaps.

### Three most important fixes

1. **Stop calling quarantine irreversible.** It is explicitly undoable elsewhere in the drafts.

   Rewrite:

   > “Quarantine is the highest-impact step, so policy requires a named human approver. It can be reversed, but it can also interrupt every tenant on that host.”

2. **Define the trusted computing boundary.** “Off the compromised network” is not enough.

   Rewrite:

   > “Claura’s local response appliance uses a separately administered management plane, independent break-glass identities, and no dependency on corporate SSO, email, or DNS. The stakeholder room connects over a pre-provisioned out-of-band path.”

3. **Treat false positives as a first-class operating risk.**

   Rewrite:

   > “Today we have not measured false-positive rates on real telemetry. Until we do, tenant failover and host quarantine default to approval; only low-blast-radius actions such as revoking one workload token may run automatically.”

---

# 2. Seed-stage VC partner

## Scores

| Draft | Score | One-sentence verdict |
|---|---:|---|
| Immune | 9/10 | Best company narrative: a narrow GPU-host escape wedge expands into an agent firewall, attestations, and cross-cloud threat intelligence. |
| Bulkheads | 8/10 | Memorable category ambition, but “become the SOLAS of AI clouds” feels grander than the demonstrated customer proof. |
| Crime Scene | 7/10 | A credible Winstead product, but expanding the War Room to every hospital, law firm, and city weakens the AI-data-center wedge. |
| Agents | 9/10 | Excellent why-now and venture-scale platform framing, although incumbents could copy the headline unless execution and data advantages are sharper. |

### First place: Immune System

It has the cleanest wedge-to-platform progression: guest-escape response → policy gate for every agent → cross-cloud immunity. That sounds more like a company than a feature bundle.

### Three most important fixes

1. **Name the first buyer and purchasing trigger.**

   Rewrite:

   > “We sell first to multi-tenant GPU clouds with 500–10,000 hosts, where one escape can expose several customers and trigger SLA, disclosure, and churn risk. The initial buyer is the CISO or head of infrastructure security.”

2. **Replace giant market statistics with a believable beachhead.**

   Rewrite:

   > “Our first market is not every data center. It is GPU clouds that run untrusted customer workloads on shared hosts and cannot staff a hyperscaler-sized incident-response team.”

3. **Make the moat concrete.**

   Rewrite:

   > “The moat is not the LLM. It is the tested containment policies, host-level integrations, incident outcome data, and signed escape fingerprints that improve across participating fleets.”

---

# 3. Winstead PC attorney

## Scores

| Draft | Score | One-sentence verdict |
|---|---:|---|
| Immune | 7/10 | Strong coordination and decision-log story, but it does not explicitly demonstrate admin-managed access or the status of compromised data. |
| Bulkheads | 7/10 | Good incident record and notification clocks, yet it openly concedes two requirements are incomplete. |
| Crime Scene | 9/10 | The most faithful response to Winstead’s clarification, with excellent treatment of hostile communications, privilege, evidentiary integrity, and human coordination. |
| Agents | 7/10 | Capable technically, but Winstead feels appended after the agent-defense story rather than built into the core pitch. |

### First place: Crime Scene

It starts from the organizer’s actual scenario rather than merely mentioning it later. The CISA example, legal channel, named decisions, “know/don’t know” brief, and evidence-preservation language all fit the track.

### Three most important fixes

1. **Explicitly demonstrate admin access management.**

   Rewrite:

   > “The incident administrator adds outside counsel as an individual, assigns the Counsel role, requires a pre-enrolled passkey, and the access change appears immediately in the audit log.”

2. **Show data scope, not merely infrastructure scope.**

   Rewrite:

   > “Current scope: one host and eleven tenant workloads. Customer dataset A: access not observed. Model registry: status unknown. File share FS-12: locked by ransomware. Egress logs: no confirmed exfiltration; investigation active.”

3. **Avoid implying that a `#legal` channel automatically creates privilege.**

   Rewrite:

   > “Counsel controls membership and distribution for the legal workspace. Claura records facts and preserves evidence; counsel—not the software—decides privilege, materiality, and notification obligations.”

---

# 4. NMC² data-center operations lead

## Scores

| Draft | Score | One-sentence verdict |
|---|---:|---|
| Immune | 9/10 | Strong end-to-end operating loop with clear warnings, blast-radius mapping, reversible containment, and fleet learning. |
| Bulkheads | 9/10 | Best operational metaphor because it centers keeping the rest of the facility running while one compartment is isolated. |
| Crime Scene | 8/10 | Excellent command coordination, but legal and communications occupy more airtime than NOC handoffs, capacity, and service impact. |
| Agents | 8/10 | Fast and technically credible, but “agents fighting agents” is less useful to operators than a calm explanation of alerts, handoffs, and workload impact. |

### First place: Bulkheads

“Seal one compartment so the hall stays up” maps naturally to data-center operations. Its explicit response ordering and “never live-migrate through an untrusted hypervisor” detail signal operational competence.

### Three most important fixes

1. **Stop saying the eleven neighbors “never stopped serving” if they restart.**

   Rewrite:

   > “Eleven neighboring workloads restarted on clean hosts. The demo shows the interruption and available spare capacity; it does not claim zero downtime.”

2. **Show the handoff explicitly.**

   Rewrite:

   > “Sentry assigns the incident to the NOC commander, Warden assigns host isolation to infrastructure security, and the next shift acknowledges the same timeline—no verbal reconstruction required.”

3. **Expose operational constraints on the approval card.**

   Rewrite:

   > “Before approval, the card shows affected tenants, estimated restart time, clean-host capacity, evidence-capture status, and the rollback path.”

---

# 5. NVIDIA developer-relations engineer

## Scores

| Draft | Score | One-sentence verdict |
|---|---:|---|
| Immune | 9/10 | Nemotron is genuinely used for multi-step reasoning and tool calls, while local execution and deterministic policy make the architecture credible. |
| Bulkheads | 8/10 | The tool-use detail is good, but the maritime story delays the proof that Nemotron is doing meaningful work. |
| Crime Scene | 8/10 | Strong real-world application and sovereign/on-prem rationale, though Nemotron is secondary to the War Room. |
| Agents | 10/10 | The clearest match to “Beyond a Chatbot”: multiple agents gather state, reason, call tools, propose actions, and produce a report. |

### First place: Agents vs. Agents

It directly answers why Nemotron is needed and shows a multi-stage tool loop rather than a conversational wrapper. The small, local model also has a convincing deployment rationale.

### Three most important fixes

1. **Show one complete live reasoning trajectory.**

   Rewrite:

   > “Triage called `get_host_inventory`, found eleven co-resident guests, called `check_reachability`, detected a management-plane path, and then wrote a scoped finding. That result changed Warden’s containment plan.”

2. **Separate model reasoning from deterministic fallback.**

   Rewrite:

   > “This row is tagged `model`; this one is tagged `fallback`. We expose the provenance so an operator can tell whether Nemotron or a deterministic playbook produced each result.”

3. **Explain why an LLM is necessary at all.**

   Rewrite:

   > “Rules recognize known indicators. Nemotron joins weak evidence across tools, determines which assets matter, and proposes a context-specific plan. It does not authorize or execute outside policy.”

---

# 6. Clockwork Labs / SpacetimeDB engineer

## Scores

| Draft | Score | One-sentence verdict |
|---|---:|---|
| Immune | 8/10 | It makes reducers and subscriptions central, but the pitch becomes disqualifying if the module is still the starter template noted in another draft’s truth check. |
| Bulkheads | 9/10 | “Ship’s log and door locks” clearly explains why state, authorization, and real-time subscriptions belong in SpacetimeDB. |
| Crime Scene | 8/10 | Strong shared-state use case, though Maincloud and live War Room claims remain conditional. |
| Agents | 9/10 | Best technical formulation—tables hold the incident, reducers referee actions, and subscriptions update every participant—but only if the implementation is demonstrable. |

### First place: Agents vs. Agents

“The database is the referee” is the clearest essential-use argument. SpacetimeDB is not merely storage; it becomes the transactional authority between probabilistic agents and consequential actions.

### Three most important fixes

1. **Prove the module, not just the UI.**

   Rewrite:

   > “This reducer receives the agent proposal, reads the policy and target rows in the same transaction, and either creates an approved action or a blocked event. Here is that exact row appearing in both consoles.”

2. **Quantify what “majority of the backend” means.**

   Rewrite:

   > “Hosts, incidents, signals, policies, proposals, approvals, reports, users, messages, tasks, and audit events all live in SpacetimeDB. The only external services are the Nemotron runner, voice proxy, and SSH bridge.”

3. **Make subscriptions visible during the demo.**

   Rewrite:

   > “I’ll send a War Room message from this phone; the judge console receives it without refresh. Now I’ll approve quarantine, and both the incident status and audit timeline update from the same committed reducer.”

---

# 7. MLH generalist judge

## Scores

| Draft | Score | One-sentence verdict |
|---|---:|---|
| Immune | 10/10 | Instantly understandable, visually demonstrable, and highly memorable, with a satisfying detect–contain–heal–remember arc. |
| Bulkheads | 8/10 | Memorable but overlong; Titanic risks becoming the thing the judge remembers instead of Claura. |
| Crime Scene | 9/10 | Excellent dramatic hook and tangible human stakes, though the product initially sounds like a secure incident chat room. |
| Agents | 9/10 | Energetic and sponsor-friendly, but “agents versus agents” is a familiar hackathon trope unless the live tool trace is exceptional. |

### First place: Immune System

It gives the judge an immediate mental model and a four-beat demo structure. “Autoimmune disease” also creates a natural explanation for the policy gate.

### Three most important fixes

1. **Reduce the feature tour.** Secure Shell and voice dilute the central wow moment.

   Rewrite:

   > “In two minutes we will show one loop: detect the escape, map its blast radius, contain it under policy, and update every responder live.”

2. **Make the before/after visual unmistakable.**

   Rewrite:

   > “Six disconnected warnings become one red incident; eleven neighboring workloads move green; the policy gate stops on amber; one human approval seals the host.”

3. **End on one line, not three concepts.**

   Rewrite:

   > “Claura: every breach makes the whole fleet immune.”

---

# 8. Non-technical first-year student judge

## Scores

| Draft | Score | One-sentence verdict |
|---|---:|---|
| Immune | 10/10 | The body metaphor explains the product without requiring knowledge of hypervisors, reducers, or incident response. |
| Bulkheads | 8/10 | The ship metaphor works, but the Titanic setup and technical mapping are too elaborate for a short table interaction. |
| Crime Scene | 9/10 | “The attacker is reading your email” is immediate and frightening, although the transition into GPU infrastructure is abrupt. |
| Agents | 8/10 | Easy to remember but potentially confusing because the audience may not understand what either set of agents is actually doing. |

### First place: Immune System

“Detect, contain, heal, remember” gives a non-technical listener the entire product in four verbs.

### Three most important fixes

1. **Translate the VM escape in one sentence.**

   Rewrite:

   > “One customer’s AI breaks out of its rented room and reaches the shared server where eleven other customers are working.”

2. **Translate the policy reducer.**

   Rewrite:

   > “The AI can recommend what to do, but a rulebook outside the AI decides what is allowed.”

3. **Avoid unexplained product names during the first minute.**

   Rewrite:

   > “Four defenders find the problem, measure who could be affected, contain it, and write the record.”

---


